<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">March across the Belts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1658">1658</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-northern-war">Second Northern War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The March across the Belts was a campaign in 1658 in which King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> led his army across the frozen straits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. The surprise attack forced Denmark to accept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a>, ceding large territories to Sweden. It marked a high point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a>'s expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/march-across-the-belts.jpg" alt="March across the Belts" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Philipp Lemke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:T%C3%A5get_%C3%B6ver_b%C3%A4lt2_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1655 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, but despite taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> he could not end the war, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> aided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a> changed sides. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> on 5 June 1657, he marched his army of about 17,000 men, only 4,000 of them Swedes, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> and took the fortress of Frederiksodde on 27 October. The Danish Straits and the Danish navy still barred the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, so in the exceptionally cold winter he waited in Jutland for the ice to thicken.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 30 January 1658 the army moved out from Brandsø onto the frozen Little Belt, between 6,000 and 7,000 men by one account and up to 12,000 by others, with the cavalry in two wings under Carl Gustaf Wrangel and the king. Dragoons with sleds carried beams and boards to bridge weak ice. The Swedes defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> at Tybrind Vig and Iversnæs, where the Danish army under Ulrik Christian Gyldenløve was short of food, clothing and experienced officers. Danish resistance on Funen collapsed; Odense fell that evening and Nyborg the next day, and Gyldenløve and much of the Danish government were captured.</p><p>From Svendborg, on the night of 6 February, between 2,000 and 3,000 cavalry led their horses across the Great Belt by way of Tåsinge and Langeland, at times through water standing on the ice, then through Lolland and Falster. They reached Zealand on 11 February, and on 15 February, with Swedish troops 22 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-of-denmark">Frederick III of Denmark</a> agreed to an unconditional peace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a>, signed in Roskilde Cathedral on 26 February 1658, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blekinge">Blekinge</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohuslan">Bohuslän</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornholm">Bornholm</a> and Trøndelag. Charles X Gustav renewed the war on 5 August 1658, aiming to end Denmark as a sovereign state, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> came to its aid, and after his death in 1660 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-copenhagen-1660">Treaty of Copenhagen</a> returned Bornholm and Trøndelag to Denmark. Swedish historians long treated the campaign as a success story, and in the age of romantic nationalism Charles X Gustav was hailed as the king who gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> its natural borders, while the part of quartermaster general Erik Dahlbergh in the decision to cross the Great Belt has long been debated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “March Across the Belts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Treaty of Roskilde (1658), imposed by Charles X Gustav after his celebrated <b>March across the Belts</b> over the frozen straits, Denmark–Norway ceded Scania, Blekinge, Halland, Bornholm, Bohuslän and Trøndelag, the region of Trondheim; Bornholm and Trøndelag were returned in 1660, but the remaining provinces fixed the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
